.TH SOLOISTD 1 "2026-11-12" "soloistd 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
soloistd \- manage multiple Spotify Soloist devices with automatic updates
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B soloistd setup
.RB [ \-\-device\-name
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-api\-key
.IR KEY ]
.RB [ \-\-instance
.IR ID ]
.RB [ \-\-pipewire\-device
.IR DEST ]
.br
.B soloistd run
.br
.B soloistd pair
.RB [ \-\-instance
.IR ID ]
.br
.B soloistd update
.br
.B soloistd ctl
.RB [ \-\-instance
.IR ID ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ ARGS .\|.\|.]
.br
.B soloistd service
.IR ACTION
.br
.B soloistd version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B soloistd
supervises one or more Spotify Soloist instances in a single systemd user
service. Each instance can have its own Spotify Connect name and PipeWire
destination. The API key stays out of the systemd unit. The daemon checks the
official Spotify CDN for new builds and recovers when a build expires.
.PP
The daemon compares the official archive's CDN metadata at startup and every
six hours. When the archive changes,
.B soloistd
downloads the stable archive for the running architecture, validates it by
executing
.BR "soloist --version" ,
atomically replaces its user\-owned copy of the executable, and restarts
all configured Soloist instances gracefully. A failed check never interrupts playback.
.PP
Spotify documents daemon exit status 10 as "build expired". That remains an
independent fallback: the current build is downloaded and Soloist restarted
whenever status 10 is reported, even if the metadata check failed.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Configure or update a device and fetch Soloist. Use --instance ID to add or
update a named instance and --pipewire-device DEST to select its PipeWire
output. The original unnamed device remains available as the default instance.
Named instances inherit the existing API key unless they set their own in the
configuration file. The interactive key prompt does not echo. The default
device name is
.IR soloistd@ <hostname>.
.TP
.B run
Run all configured instances in the foreground. This is what the user service starts.
.TP
.B pair
Replace one saved Spotify Connect session. Specify --instance ID when more than
one instance is configured. Stop the installed service first,
because Soloist permits only one process per data directory.
.TP
.B update
Fetch the current official Soloist build immediately.
.TP
.B ctl
Run a
.B soloist ctl
command against one managed Soloist process. Specify --instance ID when more
than one instance is configured.
.TP
.B service
Manage the systemd user service. The actions are
.BR install ,
.BR uninstall ,
.BR start ,
.BR stop ,
.BR restart ,
.BR status ,
and
.BR logs .
.B uninstall
removes only the unit and keeps the configuration and playback data.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and exit.
.SH MULTIPLE INSTANCES
Configure two named instances, replacing the example PipeWire destinations
with destinations on your machine:
.PP
.nf
SOLOIST_API_KEY='your-key' soloistd setup --instance kitchen \\
  --device-name Kitchen --pipewire-device alsa_output.kitchen
soloistd setup --instance bedroom --device-name Bedroom \\
  --pipewire-device alsa_output.bedroom
soloistd service install
.fi
.PP
The first command may instead follow a plain
.BR "soloistd setup" ;
that original device remains available with
.BR "--instance default" .
Instance IDs start with a letter or digit and may then contain letters,
digits, underscores, or hyphens. The ID
.B default
is reserved. A later
.B setup --instance ID
updates that instance's device name and, when supplied, its PipeWire
destination. Restart an installed service after changing its configuration.
.PP
When multiple instances are configured, specify an ID for
.B pair
and
.BR ctl ,
for example
.BR "soloistd ctl --instance kitchen status" .
Stop the service before pairing, then start it again afterward. Each named
instance has its own session and cache directory. Custom
.B data_dir
and
.B cache_dir
paths must not overlap between instances. Give each instance a distinct
.B websocket
listen address if that option is configured.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOLOIST_API_KEY
Supplies the API key to
.BR "soloistd setup" .
Use the interactive setup prompt to avoid typing the key into shell history.
.TP
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ", " XDG_DATA_HOME ", " XDG_CACHE_HOME
Locate the files below. The usual
.IR ~/.config ,
.IR ~/.local/share ,
and
.I ~/.cache
fallbacks are used when they are unset.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/soloistd/config.json
Private configuration, mode 0600 in a directory of mode 0700.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/soloistd/bin/soloist
Managed Soloist executable.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/soloistd/release.json
Metadata for the installed release.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/soloist
Spotify session and state for the original unnamed instance.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/soloist
Playback cache for the original unnamed instance.
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/soloistd/instances/ID
State for a named instance.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/soloistd/ID
Cache for a named instance.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/systemd/user/soloistd.service
Generated user unit. It contains only the path to
.BR soloistd ,
never the API key.
.SH SECURITY
The generated unit never contains the API key. Soloist itself requires the key
as a command\-line argument, so the key can still be visible to same\-user
process inspection while Soloist is running.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B soloistd
exits 0 on success, 2 on a usage error, and otherwise propagates the exit
status of the command it ran.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
.PP
Soloist documentation:
.UR https://developer.spotify.com/documentation/soloist
.UE
